@using Memoria.Web.Extensibility

@* What one uploaded archive brought, over the table it was listed in: each assembly file in it and,
   under each, the domain types registered from that file, grouped the way the Types tab counts
   them.

   Open because the address says so and closed by a link that takes that back, like the JSON pop-up
   over a page of the log. No script and no <dialog>: the settings page renders statically, the
   framework's modal needs JavaScript to open at all, and a reader who followed a link here can
   leave the way they came — the close is a link, the backdrop behind it is the same link, and the
   browser's own back button undoes the whole thing.

   Its own component rather than markup on the settings page, because that page styles the two
   <dialog> elements it does open by script under the same class name the overlay's sheet carries,
   and a sheet drawn there would be drawn twice over. *@
<div class="overlay" id="@Anchor" tabindex="-1">
    @* The way out that a click anywhere outside is. Kept out of the reading order and out of the
       accessibility tree: it is the same address as the Close beside the title, and offered twice
       it would be read out twice. *@
    <a class="overlay-dismiss" href="@CloseLink" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"></a>

    @* Named by its heading rather than marked aria-modal: nothing here traps focus or hides the
       page behind it from a screen reader, and saying otherwise would promise a reader something
       that is not true. Narrow, like the payload sheet: a list of names is read down, not across. *@
    <section class="dialog dialog-narrow" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="@(Anchor)-title">
        <header class="dialog-header">
            @* The same mark the column that opened this is marked with, so what was clicked and
               what arrived look like the same thing — and the site's mark for a declaration, which
               is what a list of types is a list of. *@
            <h2 id="@(Anchor)-title">
                <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Types"/>
                @Archive.Name
            </h2>

            <a class="dialog-close" href="@CloseLink" aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</a>
        </header>

        <div class="dialog-body">
            @if (Archive.Assemblies.Count == 0)
            {
                <p class="none">No assemblies in it.</p>
            }
            else
            {
                <ul class="assemblies">
                    @foreach (var assembly in Archive.Assemblies)
                    {
                        var registered = Catalogue.RegisteredFrom(assembly);

                        <li>
                            <span class="assembly">@assembly</span>

                            @* One sentence rather than a blank, because a file that registered
                               nothing is the case worth noticing: a dependency the domain needs,
                               or an assembly that did not load — both listed on the same terms as
                               the one that brought everything, and stepped in where its list of
                               kinds would start so the two answers stand in the same place. *@
                            @if (registered.Count == 0)
                            {
                                <p class="none">No domain types registered from it.</p>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <ul class="kinds">
                                    @foreach (var kind in registered)
                                    {
                                        <li>
                                            <span class="kind">@kind.Label</span>
                                            <ul class="types">
                                                @foreach (var type in kind.Types)
                                                {
                                                    @* Named as the index pages name it: by what it
                                                       is bound as where it is bound, and by the
                                                       class otherwise. *@
                                                    <li>@DomainTypeDescriber.LabelOf(type)<ObsoleteMark Type="type"/></li>
                                                }
                                            </ul>
                                        </li>
                                    }
                                </ul>
                            }
                        </li>
                    }
                </ul>
            }
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {

    /// <summary>The archive whose contents are being read.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public InstalledArchive Archive { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>The types currently registered, which is where each file's are looked up.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DomainTypeCatalogue Catalogue { get; set; } = DomainTypeCatalogue.Empty;

    /// <summary>The page with nothing asked for, which is what closes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string CloseLink { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>
    /// What this is called in the address, so the link that opens it can land on it.
    /// </summary>
    public const string Anchor = "types";

}
